"Delete the StatBlock type entirely: its logic divides among four INDEPENDENT group blocks, each its own struct AND its own runtime component(s), with no parent/aggregate type, no composition, and no 'stat block application' step — the per-group total components ARE the applied/read state. Accept incidental repetition between similarly-behaving groups. GROUPS: STATS (mhp, mep, attack, defense, size) — every physical object has hp/size/defense; APPEARANCE (appearance_feature_ids) — every physical object has these; BODY-CAPACITY (hand, body, relic) — the ONLY group whose change dirties equipment computation, which still drops capacity-violating items (apply-if-fits, unchanged from today); READINESS (morale, bladed, blunt, pole, ward, focus, gait, reach, wing, upright, fang, claw, ooze, and any further tag that suggests a kind of action is available) — only meaningful on entities with an action component. Sources (baseline/traits/status/quest/equipment/stance) are the ONLY layer that mixes groups: each holds per-group Option data and, on change, dirties only the group flags where its Option is Some (value-diff skips unchanged); below the source layer each group flows independently via automatic per-group dirty flags. Assets are authored PER-GROUP (deliberately not flat), so each entity type declares only the groups meaningful to it, and stats that change together stay together. action_ids stops being stored stat data and becomes a DERIVED set (readiness-permitted candidates selected into customizations; see child)."